data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**Recommendation:** keep `mode` as an explicit dependency of `alerts.ts`. Read `emergencyMode` from `config.ts` once, at the worker entry point or composition root, and pass it in.\n\n**Why explicit beats importing config directly:**\n- **Purity and testability:** `alerts` stays a function of its inputs. Tests just pass `mode` and don't need to mock modules, set env vars, or reset config between cases.\n- **Runtime changes:** if `emergencyMode` can ever change while the worker runs (flag refresh, admin toggle), passing it per call lets each run use the current value. A module-level import tends to freeze whatever value it saw at load.\n- **Coupling:** if `alerts` doesn't import config, you avoid hidden global state and import-order problems, and `alerts` can be reused by other callers.\n- **Clear ownership:** `config.ts` owns parsing and validation. `alerts` owns the alerting logic.\n\n**When importing directly is fine:** the value really never changes after startup, `alerts` has only one caller, and passing it would mean threading `mode` through many layers. Even then, I'd use a default parameter (`mode = config.emergencyMode`) rather than reading config inside the function body, so tests can still override it.\n\n**Shape:** use a narrow type like `type AlertMode = \"normal\" | \"emergency\"` (or a boolean), not the whole config object. Then `alerts` depends only on what it needs.\n\n**Test strategy:**\n- **Unit tests for `alerts`:** pass each mode explicitly and check the behavior differences, such as routing, severity, throttling or suppression, and message content. Assert on an injected notifier or sink, not on a real side effect. No module mocking needed.\n- **Unit tests for `config.ts`:** check that env or file input maps to `emergencyMode` correctly. Cover the default, the truthy and falsy forms you accept, and invalid values (fail fast or fall back, whichever you choose).\n- **One wiring test at the composition root:** with config set to emergency, confirm the worker calls `alerts` with emergency mode. This is the only test that touches both modules, and it catches the \"forgot to pass it\" bug.\n- **Type-level guard:** make `mode` a required parameter, not optional, so the compiler flags call sites that skip it.\n- **If mode can change at runtime:** add a test that flips the config value between two invocations and checks that `alerts` sees the new value.\n\nOnce you're ready to implement, I can sketch the signatures."}}

